<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Langensalza (1866)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Langensalza was fought on 27 June 1866 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. The Hanoverian army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> defeated a Prussian force, but surrounded by larger Prussian armies it surrendered two days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> then annexed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-langensalza-1866.jpg" alt="Battle of Langensalza (1866)" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg von Boddien (+1928) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Langensalza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> approached in 1866, most of the kingdoms around Prussia sided with Austria for fear of losing their autonomy. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v-of-hanover">George V of Hanover</a> lost time trying to negotiate separately with both powers, and his foreign minister declared that Bismarck would never break the rule of a six-week interval before an invasion. On 15 June 1866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> to disarm at once, and the next day Prussian troops, those of General August Karl von Goeben among them, moved against all three.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Hanoverian army happened to be mobilised for its summer exercises. George sent its 19,000 men under General Alexander von Arentschildt marching south to join their Bavarian allies, while some 40,000 Prussians entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in four detachments. Falckenstein took the undefended capital, and Helmuth von Moltke closed a box around the Hanoverians, with Beyer to the south and Flies, with 9,000 troops, to the west. Ordered to hold fast and stop any breakout, Flies instead attacked. After a feint towards Thamsbruck he struck at Merxleben, but the larger Hanoverian force and its artillery drove his men back towards Langensalza, routing them and taking more than 900 prisoners.</p><p>The victory nevertheless halted the Hanoverian march and let the Prussian forces from north and south converge. Pushed east, further from their allies, and pinned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz Mountains</a>, the Hanoverians surrendered at Nordhausen two days later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The quick occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, took the Austrians by surprise and weakened their position, since the small states could together have given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> more than 100,000 troops. The Royal Hanoverian Army ceased to exist. Langensalza also saw the first actual combat mission of the Red Cross, created under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention">First Geneva Convention</a> of 1864: 30 trained volunteer nurses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, with Prussian medical personnel wearing the sign of the Red Cross on their arms, tended the wounded on the Prussian side.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Langensalza (1866)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His army won at <b>Langensalza</b> but surrendered two days later.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a></li></ul></section>
